\pard \s40 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1  (id)\b initWithBitmapDataPlanes:\b0 (unsigned char **)\i planes \b \i0 pixelsWide:\b0 (int)\i width \b \i0 pixelsHigh:\b0 (int)\i height \b \i0 bitsPerSample:\b0 (int)\i bps \b \i0 samplesPerPixel:\b0 (int)\i spp \b \i0 hasAlpha:\b0 (BOOL)\i alpha \b \i0 isPlanar:\b0 (BOOL)\i isPlanar \b \i0 colorSpaceName:\b0 (NSString *)\i colorSpaceName \b \i0 bytesPerRow:\b0 (int)\i rowBytes \b \i0 bitsPerPixel:\b0 (int)\i pixelBits\i0  \
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Initializes the receiver, a newly allocated NSBitmapImageRep object, so that it can render the image specified in \i planes\i0  and described by the other arguments. If the object can\'27t be initialized, this method frees it and returns \b nil\b0 . Otherwise, it returns the object (\b self\b0 ).\
\fs14\
\fs28 \i planes\i0  is an array of character pointers, each of which points to a buffer containing raw image data. If the data is in planar configuration, each buffer holds one component\'d0one plane\'d0of the data. Color planes are arranged in the standard order\'d0for example, red before green before blue for RGB color. All color planes precede the coverage plane.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If the data is in meshed configuration (\i isPlanar\i0  is NO), only the first buffer is read.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If \i planes\i0  is NULL or if it\'27s an array of NULL pointers, this method allocates enough memory to hold the image described by the other arguments. You can then obtain pointers to this memory (with the \b getBitmapDataPlanes:\b0  or \b bitmapData\b0  method) and fill in the image data. In this case, the allocated memory will belong to the object and will be freed when it\'27s freed.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If \i planes\i0  is not NULL and the array contains at least one data pointer, the object will only reference the image data; it won\'27t copy it. The buffers won\'27t be freed when the object is\'80freed.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Each of the other arguments (besides \i planes\i0 ) informs the NSBitmapImageRep object about the image. They\'27re explained below:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab \i width\i0  and \i height\i0  specify the size of the image in pixels. The size in each direction must be greater than 0.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab \i bps\i0  (bits per sample) is the number of bits used to specify one pixel in a single component of the data. All components are assumed to have the same bits per sample.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab \i spp\i0  (samples per pixel) is the number of data components. It includes both color components and the coverage component (alpha), if present. Meaningful values range from 1 through 5. An image with cyan, magenta, yellow, and black (CMYK) color components plus a coverage component would have an \i spp\i0  of 5; a gray-scale image that lacks a coverage component would have an \i spp\i0  of 1.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab \i alpha\i0  should be YES if one of the components counted in the number of samples per pixel (\i spp\i0 ) is a coverage component, and NO if there is no coverage component.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab \i isPlanar\i0  should be YES if the data components are laid out in a series of separate \'aaplanes\'ba or channels (\'aaplanar configuration\'ba), and NO if component values are interwoven in a single channel (\'aameshed configuration\'ba).\
\fs14\
\s5 \fi0 \fs28 For example, in meshed configuration, the red, green, blue, and coverage values for the first pixel of an image would precede the red, green, blue, and coverage values for the second pixel, and so on. In planar configuration, red values for all the pixels in the image would precede all green values, which would precede all blue values, which would precede all coverage values. \
\fs14\
\s6 \fi-298 \fs28 \'b7\tab \i colorSpaceName\i0  indicates how data values are to be interpreted. It should be one of the following enumerated values:\
\fs14\
\pard \s61 \li6753 \fi-4838 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9525 \fs28 NSCalibratedWhiteColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSCalibratedBlackColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSCalibratedRGBColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSDeviceWhiteColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSDeviceBlackColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSDeviceRGBColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSDeviceCMYKColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSNamedColorSpace\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSCustomColorSpace\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab \i rowBytes\i0  is the number of bytes that are allocated for each scan line in each plane of data. A scan line is a single row of pixels spanning the width of the image.\
\fs14\
\s5 \fi0 \fs28 Normally, \i rowBytes\i0  can be figured from the \i width\i0  of the image, the number of bits per pixel in each sample (\i bps\i0 ), and, if the data is in a meshed configuration, the number of samples per pixel (\i spp\i0 ). However, if the data for each row is aligned on word or other boundaries, it may have been necessary to allocate more memory for each row than there is data to fill it. \i rowBytes\i0  lets the object know whether that\'27s the case. If \i rowBytes\i0  is 0, the NSBitmapImageRep assumes that there\'27s no empty space at the end of a row.\
\fs14\
\s6 \fi-298 \fs28 \'b7\tab \i pixelBits\i0  informs the NSBitmapImageRep how many bits are actually allocated per pixel in each plane of data. If the data is in planar configuration, this normally equals \i bps\i0  (bits per sample). If the data is in meshed configuration, it normally equals \i bps\i0  times \i spp\i0  (samples per pixel). However, it\'27s possible for a pixel specification to be followed by some meaningless bits (empty space), as may happen, for example, if pixel data is aligned on byte boundaries. NSBitmapImageRep supports only a limited number of \i pixelBits\i0  values (other than the default): for RGB images with 12 \i bps\i0 , \i pixelBits\i0  may be 16; for RGB images with 24 \i bps\i0 , \i pixelBits\i0  may be 32. The legal values for \i pixelBits\i0  are system dependent.\
\fs14\
\s5 \fi0 \fs28 If \i pixelBits\i0  is 0, the object will interpret the number of bits per pixel to be the expected value, without any meaningless bits.\
\fs14\
